{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And you shall place.</b> The tablets are the testimony, as if they are like a marriage contract. They are the tablets of the covenant and the faithful witness. \"And he placed the testimony into the ark\" (below 40:20). Furthermore, Moses did not write a scroll of the Torah [before this point]; rather, the priests placed the scroll of the Torah at the side of the ark of the covenant of YHWH, outside. And furthermore it is written: \"There was nothing in the ark except the two stone tablets\" (1 Kings 8:9). And there is a dispute.\n\nOne may ask: Scripture said to Moses, \"And you shall place into the ark the tablets that I shall give you,\" and it is written, \"And he gave to Moses…\" (below 31:18) — yet he broke them and did not fulfill the command of HaShem. One may answer: perhaps for this reason our sages said that the fragments of the tablets are placed in the ark, and Scripture does not contradict their words, for both the first and the second are tablets of the covenant. And furthermore we may answer that Scripture speaks only of the second tablets, and there is no objection from the word \"that I shall give you\" (v. 21), for behold it is also written of them: \"and YHWH gave them to me\" (Deuteronomy 10:4). And furthermore, we do not find that HaShem said to Moses to make an ark and place the first tablets there, for HaShem knew that Moses would break them; rather, He said concerning the tablets that Moses hewed: \"and you shall make yourself an ark of wood\" (Deuteronomy 10:1). And our predecessors said that this is not the ark that Bezalel made — and it is correct. And by way of reason it is not far from my eyes, that he speaks of the ark that Bezalel made, as it is written: \"and they shall make an ark of acacia wood\" (above v. 10). And there is no reason to ask where these tablets would be placed until the Tabernacle was erected, for there is no need to mention this.\n\nAnd I needed this interpretation because of his mention of \"and I placed the tablets in the ark that I made\" (Deuteronomy 10:5). Now if we say that he recounts the matter as it happened — even though we do not know the reason why he mentions this — then what do we make of what is written afterward: \"and they were there, as HaShem commanded me\" (ibid.)? This is a sign that he speaks of the tablets that Moses placed in the ark of the covenant, and they are there \"to this day.\" And this is the meaning of \"and they were there to this day\" (1 Kings 8:8) — also as a sign and testimony that the scroll of the Torah was not in the ark, for at first he placed the tablets in the ark, and afterward the cover with the cherubim. And behold, the Glory dwells upon the cherubim, and it is written: \"who sits upon the cherubim\" (Psalms 80:2). If so, how could Moses place the scroll of the Torah in the fortieth year inside the ark, if he does not remove the cover with the cherubim from atop the ark? And there is no need for all this:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The colon at the end is literal; Ibn Ezra concludes by dismissing the entire line of reasoning as unnecessary."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:38:24.868657182Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}